				\begin{document}$${\bar{T}}_{{{\mathrm{BGC}}}}$$\end{document}T¯BGC, calculated following the methods of Amali et al.42 and using the TCRE for CESM2 calculated by Arora et al.43 (see “Methods”), shows significant global-mean cooling for all scenarios, with the rate increasing from mid-century onwards (Fig. 2b). This reflects sustained land carbon uptake by new forests (350 GtCO2 in the Bastin scenario, 435 GtCO2 in the Moustakis scenario and 220 GtCO2 in the Hurtt scenario; Fig. S1), primarily by vegetation, followed by woody debris. This carbon sequestration potential is in the mid-range of total estimates for global sequestration potential from reforestation and would equate to around 5–10 times the present-day annual global CO2 emissions. By the end of the century (mean over 2086–2100), the Moustakis scenario leads to the most substantial BGC cooling (−0.25 °C), followed by Bastin scenario (−0.22 °C) and Hurtt scenario (−0.13 °C). The sustained cooling trend, which persists after reforestation stops in 2070, suggests that the full BGC effect takes a long time to establish due to sustained carbon uptake of vegetation and woody debris, and has not been realized by the end of the simulations in 2100. A critical implication for our results is that the continued establishment of the BGC effect beyond 2100 would likely cause the long-term total climate efficacy of reforestation to be greater than our end-of-century projections suggest. However, the eventual long-term efficacy beyond 2100 depends also on the balance between this continued carbon sink and potential counteracting processes, such as reduced carbon uptake efficiency due to lower atmospheric CO2 and increased carbon losses from climate extremes like droughts.

Since our simulations are concentration-driven and solely capture the biogeophysical effects of reforestation, we approximate the associated biogeochemical temperature effect based on changes in land carbon stocks. This approximation uses the concept of the transient climate response to cumulative emissions (TCRE)42,51,64. The TCRE quantifies the amount of global warming per unit of cumulative fossil fuel emission at the point of doubling of atmospheric CO2 concentration relative to a baseline. A key characteristic of the TCRE is its approximate constancy over time and independence from the specific emission trajectory65. To estimate the global-mean near-surface temperature change attributable to the biogeochemical impacts of reforestation (\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
